These are all good questions. I will say that there is another sorta similar situation where Visionary has a quote in one comic directly before her first apperance. So she has a quote box in one comic, and in the very next one you see her for the first time and that is attributed to her first apperance.With that said, it&#39;s very possible that we as consumers of the comics see text bubbles of apostate speaking but do not actually see him until Fanatic 25.&nbsp;I doubt there are 24 whole issues of &quot;Fanatic&quot; that <em>just</em> detail backstory until the point she gains her armor and sword. That theory seems <em>highly</em> unlikely.&nbsp;Here is how it went down.Fanatic is<em> </em>first revealed in Mystery Comics where this whole storyline begins. The character is introduced at the begining of her major story arc. Apostate is quoted a lot through this time but is never seen, a mystery voice from behind the curtain. Mystery Comics wouldnt be named that if there were not mysteries to solve. Fanatic then proceedes to gets her own book which continues the storyline setup in Mystery Comics up until Fanatic 25 where Apostate, her nemesis, is finally revealed on the pages and they meet face to face for the first time. Fanatic 25-29 likely detail their major encounter together into the aftermath. Fanatic 30 is the first apperance of Redeemer Fanatic which follows their first encounter.Where do you get the idea that there must be 2 years between Mystery Comic 368 amd Fanatic 25? You cant assume standard publication rates because different comics come out at different rates and are not always consistent even within those rates for Sentinels Comics.Also, it makes perfect sense to me that when you introduce a new character, you would do it in a comic that already has a fan base. So you introduce Fanatic and her whole mystery for a few issues in Mystery Comics until you give her her own book to continue that story after you have grabbed initial readers. Keep in mind this is also a fictional comic and &quot;business sense&quot; doesn&#39;t hold much weight to shoot down theories. Even so, in this case I feel like the timeline does make business sense regardless.

[/quote]

I think in many of its points, your theory and (partially) my theory #1 are pretty close. I agree with you entirely that my theory #2 isn&#39;t a super likely theory - I included it only because it was a possible theory, in the interests of completeness.

While it is true that different comics come out at different rates, comics that come out more frequently than once per month are pretty unusual. I&#39;ve been out of the game for a bit, but the last weekly comic I can remember was &quot;52&quot;, which was in part so named specifically because it is so unusual for a comic to come out all 52 weeks of one year.&nbsp; While we do know that one of the &quot;rules&quot; of the game is that dif  ferent comics can have different publication rates, I would expect that if this whole thing was truly thought out from the beginning (which is one of the things that we have always been given to believe), then I expect a reasonable explanation for why Fanatic would be published so much more frequently than the &quot;industry-standard&quot; normal publication rate (which seems pretty Sentinel Comics standard, too, if its been publishing since WWII, and the high issue numbers are in the 600s-700s). Otherwise different publication rates is just a deus ex machina that fixes a story paying off 24 issues after it starts, with substantial stuff left unresolved (the appearance of Apostate), and seemingly nothing related in-between.&nbsp; 

Even if Fanatic is a weekly comic, you have her first appearance in MC 368, and then 24 issues later (six months), you finally have the payoff of the &quot;man in the shadows&quot; from MC 367-368. It&#39;s possible, but seems like a long time to wait. Again, I&#39;m not saying that this theory is definitely wrong because if this - I think it&#39;s the best theory going - I&#39;m just saying it&#39;s got enough that doesn&#39;t feel quite right that it&#39;s possible that it&#39;s wrong.&nbsp;

I completely agree with your reading of events from Fanatic 25 up through Fanatic 30, and think that it makes total sense. I am leaning very strongly towards whatever happens in MC not being a physical confrontation directly between Apostate and Fanatic. To make a quick left turn, in Sunrise, the &quot;sets&quot; of Citizens all have quotes/arts in the same issue of Sunrise together. I suspect that this is done to tell us the story that our heroes are meeting/fighting the &quot;sets&quot; of Citizens in waves up to fighting the big bad - Citizen Dawn. I think that the same thing may be happening here. &nbsp;We see a bunch of relics and demons - this may be the &quot;softening up&quot; of Fanatic.

That said, however, there is the problem that Apostate does appear personally in several arts in MC, other than just Smite the Transgressor, which has been explicitly disavowed. He also physically appears on Fallen Angel (369), Reactive Plating (370) and Karmic Retribution (370). That&#39;s a lot of &quot;oh, that&#39;s just art not matching up&quot; - showing up on three cards that quote two issues. If you really want Apostate to not show up in MC, why didn&#39;t &gt;G just make the quotes reference Fanatic issues? I do feel a bit like it strains the theory of &quot;he doesn&#39;t really show up in MC&quot; to have that many art appearances in two consecutive books.

I still think its the best theory we have going - it just has too much against it for me to feel certain it&#39;s right.&nbsp;

